---

<section class="accelerating">
  <div class="accelerating__inner" data-reveal>
    <div class="accelerating__copy">
      <h2 class="accelerating__heading">Accelerating Productivity</h2>

      <p class="accelerating__body">
        CHIME is the 'industry first' application for network operators, designed to improve field
        force productivity while addressing the industry-wide challenge of increasing regulatory
        volumes. With over 25 years of experience and a dedicated focus on network operators since
        2014, we understand the industry challenges, and ensure every aspect of CHiME is designed to
        meet these unique challenges.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="accelerating__media">
      <picture>
        <source srcset="/images/accelerating-network.avif" type="image/avif" />
        <img src="/images/accelerating-network.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </picture>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .accelerating {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin-inline: 1.25rem;
    border-radius: 2rem;
    background: linear-gradient(120deg, var(--color-blue-deep), var(--color-blue-mid) 130%);
    color: var(--color-white);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .accelerating {
      margin-inline: 2rem;
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 1200px) {
    .accelerating {
      margin-inline: 5rem;
      border-radius: 2.5rem;
    }
  }


  /* Two columns, both stretched to the same height: copy (heading pinned to
     the top, paragraph pinned to the bottom, via space-between) alongside
     an image column running the FULL height of the card - not a small
     icon tucked in a corner with dead space around it. */
  .accelerating__inner {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.3fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    align-items: stretch;
    gap: 3rem;
    min-height: 75svh;
    padding: 2em;
  }

  .accelerating__copy {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 2.5rem;
  }

  .accelerating__heading {
    font-size: clamp(2em, 4vw, 3em);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-white);
  }

  .accelerating__body {
    max-width: 32rem;
    font-size: 1.125em;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
  }

  .accelerating__media {
    position: relative;
    border-radius: 1.5rem;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* Absolutely positioned + inset: 0, not width/height: 100% on a
     statically positioned img - verified directly that height: 100%
     was silently not resolving at all here: the img was falling back to
     its own intrinsic aspect ratio (rendering at exactly width / the
     image's own natural ratio, ignoring the container completely). That
     matches a known CSS edge case - when a flex item's height comes from
     min-height clamping a zero flex-basis (mobile's flex: 1 below,
     rather than an explicit height), that's not always treated as
     "definite" enough for a descendant's percentage height to resolve
     against. Absolute positioning sizes against the nearest positioned
     ancestor's box directly and isn't subject to that same restriction. */
  .accelerating__media img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    /* A single-column grid auto-sizes each row to its own content, so
       fixing the image to aspect-ratio: 4/3 (the previous approach) left
       it as a small boxed photo with the card's own background showing
       as dead space above and below it - the opposite of the "full
       height, not a small icon with dead space" intent already stated
       above for the desktop columns. flex: 1 is the direct mobile
       equivalent of that same intent: on desktop, align-items: stretch
       makes the image match the text column's height with no fixed
       number involved anywhere; here, flex: 1 makes it consume whatever
       height the copy text doesn't need out of .accelerating__inner's
       own min-height: 75svh, which is exactly the same "stretch to fill,
       no fixed ratio" mechanism turned 90 degrees for a stacked layout. */
    .accelerating__inner {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
    }

    /* flex: 1 alone collapses this to ~24px: in a narrow single-column
       layout the body paragraph needs more vertical space than
       .accelerating__inner's own min-height: 75svh on its own, so there's
       nothing left for flex: 1 to hand the image (verified directly -
       this isn't a guess). min-height here is a floor against total
       collapse, not a locked proportion like aspect-ratio - flex: 1 still
       drives every bit of growth above it from whatever space the copy
       text actually leaves. */
    .accelerating__media {
      order: -1;
      flex: 1;
      min-height: 18rem;
    }
  }
</style>
